    "answer": {
      "short": "Yes. Expanding the cube and rewriting the mixed terms using root-coefficient relationships gives the sum of cubes, but the working is lengthy and error-prone.\n\nA more direct method is to substitute each root into the cubic and add the three equations.",
      "worked": "Yes. The expansion can be rearranged as\n$$\\alpha^3+\\beta^3+\\gamma^3=(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)^3-3(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)(\\alpha\\beta+\\alpha\\gamma+\\beta\\gamma)+3\\alpha\\beta\\gamma.$$\nEach quantity on the right can be found from the coefficients, so Bob's method is valid, although lengthy and error-prone.\n\nThe taught direct method is to substitute each root into $ax^3+bx^2+cx+d=0$ and add the equations:\n$$a(\\alpha^3+\\beta^3+\\gamma^3)+b(\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2)+c(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)+3d=0.$$\nUse the known sum of roots and sum of squares, then rearrange for the sum of cubes.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
